A Heat Transfer Textbook — Lienhard and Lienhard (@th-lienhard: 0, is #reference, #part-of @th-sources) { r_7TKQ2D5YRNQ8XVHXNJQDRANWKD
John H. Lienhard V and John H. Lienhard IV. A Heat Transfer Textbook, sixth edition, Phlogiston Press, 2024, version 6.00 dated 19 April 2024. [Author's MIT site](https://ahtt.mit.edu/) · [Author PDF](https://ahtt.mit.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/AHTTv600.pdf) · [MIT teaching reading context](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-51-intermediate-heat-and-mass-transfer-fall-2008/pages/readings/).

Read the entire book, including its mathematical developments, worked examples, substantial independent problems and mass-transfer treatment. Use it continuously through conduction, convection, phase-change heat transfer, radiation and coupled transport; later investigations return to complete relevant chapters rather than treating a correlation table as a substitute for theory.

Indexed primary title-page metadata confirms the sixth edition and version date. Direct automated index/PDF retrieval repeatedly timed out or returned 502 on 29 October 2026; complete page inspection is not claimed. The authors authorize personal and nonprofit instructional downloading/printing, while retaining copyright and restrictions on other uses. Free reading is not a general CC adaptation licence. Follow the author for corrections and actual file revisions; keep the complete text external.

**Return:** [Thermodynamics and heat transfer sources](../Sources.lingua#r_4N96T70VYW3C3M91M2SXF5KEQF).

**Third engineering-core access check — 31 October 2026:** the author site again timed out. The accessible [MIT 2.51 reading page](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-51-intermediate-heat-and-mass-transfer-fall-2008/pages/readings/) names Fourier conduction, thermal resistance and lumped capacity among background topics and identifies the third edition used in Fall 2008. It is not an inspection of the sixth-edition pages or a new licence grant. The full-book assignment and prior access/copyright qualification remain. The common network derivations and examples are original; OpenStax and MIT 18.03SC provide accessible introductory reading.

